WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:03.770
Thank you.

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:04.270
Thank you Hadley.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:04.770
Thank you.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:14.180
The title of this
talk is RStudio 2022.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:17.620
But the actual
subject of the talk

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:20.740
is to consider the
question of what would it

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:26.120
take for us to give this
same talk in 2122?

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.370
That's what we're going to
try to consider here today.

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:32.950
And to do that, we want to
start by reflecting back

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:34.390
a little bit on
where we've been.

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:37.930
And I want to point out now that
if you go to a tech conference

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:40.060
and the founders are
on stage and they

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.640
say we have some exciting
news, and the first slide says,

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.460
our journey so far there's a
95% chance the company's

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:46.510
getting acquired.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:50.920
So that is not at all
what's happening today

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.090
and that will never happen.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:55.850
So, yeah, that might have
occurred to some of you.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:01:00.790
So anyway, we are feeling
incredibly blessed

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:02.450
to be part of this community.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:06.190
And you know, the company
started 13 years ago,

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:09.550
but many of us have actually
been working on and with R

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:11.380
far longer than that.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:14.950
And we've built a set
of tools and software

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:19.870
that helps people ask and answer
the hardest questions with code

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:23.440
in a reproducible fashion,
and all the while

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.630
creating an incredible
community, which

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:27.190
I think if you're
here today, you

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:30.670
feel if you've been
at past confs, meetups, all

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.167
the interactions
we have online, I

00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:34.000
think the community
we've built together is,

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.770
is really remarkable.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:38.840
And also I wanted to
reflect back on what

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.580
the most difficult
problem that we've

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:42.690
solved so far as a company.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.290
And one very obvious
answer to this question,

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.510
and it is a
difficult problem, is

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:51.530
that we've made R usable and
productive and approachable

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:53.480
for many, many,
many, many people,

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.550
many more people than
probably any of us

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.890
thought would be possible.

00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:59.350
And that is a difficult problem.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:00.600
It's a very important problem.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.600
It kind of enables all
the things I talked about

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:03.570
on the first slide.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:09.139
But when me and Hadley and Joe
and others all got together,

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:11.630
all got together
about 10 years ago

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:15.395
and said, hey, let's work on
this together for a long time,

00:02:15.395 --> 00:02:17.270
I actually was pretty
confident that we would

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:20.450
be able to solve this problem.

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:24.410
But there's another problem
that I think we have solved,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:27.920
which I wasn't 100% confident
we'd be able to solve.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:30.380
I hoped, but I was
100% confident,

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:34.400
which is to create a sustainable
model for funding open source

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.110
scientific software.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:39.290
Scientific software is
traditionally created

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:42.470
by large proprietary
software vendors,

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:45.380
kind of necessarily so in a
way, because it's actually

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:50.870
there's a lot of software to be
written about, a lot of people

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.060
depend on it.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:54.950
But we really felt
strongly that we'd

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:58.910
like to find a way to make open
source software for science

00:02:58.910 --> 00:02:59.656
sustainable.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.350
And reflecting a little bit
on why this problem matters.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.700
I think actually everyone here
has a pretty good intuition

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.540
about why this problem matters.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:15.220
And that was a subject of a talk
I gave a couple of years ago.

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:17.410
OK OK.

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:18.270
Use this instead.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.170
OK, great.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:23.740
As a subject of a talk I
gave a couple of years ago,

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:26.230
reflecting a little bit on
the most important dimensions

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:30.490
of that, the scientific process
and the scientific method

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:33.860
depends on replication
and reproducibility.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:36.910
And fundamentally, if I can't
run your software either

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:38.620
over space, you
know, a colleague

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.170
in another company or another
country or another university,

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:43.750
or over time, if I
can't run your software,

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:46.560
I can't reproduce your work
and I can't build on your work.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.360
So having the fundamental
software be free and open

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.810
source kind of
ensures or at least

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:56.530
creates the conditions for
widespread reproducibility

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:59.930
and replicability
and also resiliency.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:01.490
Software does die.

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:02.660
Companies die.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:04.400
Versions of products die.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.160
Things get out of fashion.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.290
Open source software has
that fundamental right

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:12.640
to fork, which means that
software can persist even

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:17.140
beyond the time period over
which its main sponsors are

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:18.310
working on it.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:21.860
And then finally, participation
you've seen with CRAN.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:24.280
Tens of thousands of
packages addressing

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:29.410
all different domains,
methodological approaches

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:32.675
and having the user community
be part of building the tools

00:04:32.675 --> 00:04:34.300
and deciding what
methods are supported

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:37.790
is fundamentally better than
the idea of a single vendor

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:39.790
or a couple of vendors
being a choke point, sort

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:42.490
of deciding what an analytic
methods people are going to be

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:43.720
able to use with the software.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.840
So I think this problem
matters a great deal

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:50.950
to science, data science,
science, the future of science.

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:52.960
And so when we think
about the solving

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:57.790
this problem of sustainable
funding for open source

00:04:57.790 --> 00:05:00.380
scientific software, there's a
few dimensions of the problem.

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:04.042
Some of it has to do with just
the mission of the entities

00:05:04.042 --> 00:05:05.000
that are working on it.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:06.880
Some of it actually has to do
with the way incentives are

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:08.180
aligned around the work.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.600
And then and then even if
you have those things right,

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.520
getting to a certain
scale of effort

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.820
is actually another
threshold condition.

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:21.040
Companies like Wolfram
and MathWorks and SAS,

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:22.570
you know, employ
hundreds of people

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:24.170
to work on scientific software.

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:27.910
And I think something
along that those lines

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:30.490
is, is probably necessary
to do it really well

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:32.260
with open source.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:35.290
So first kind of the
corporate charter

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:37.945
and again we talked about this
a couple of years ago at conf

00:05:37.945 --> 00:05:40.570
and it's important to note that
when we became a public benefit

00:05:40.570 --> 00:05:43.480
corporation, we actually
encoded our mission

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:45.080
into the corporate charter.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:49.150
And it actually is the case
that our directors and officers

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:52.660
have a fiduciary duty
to pursue this mission.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:54.610
And that mission,
when we wrote it,

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:57.320
I think about three years
ago, was actually written.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:59.200
You'll notice here
pretty broadly,

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:01.120
we talk about free and
open source software

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:03.370
for data science,
scientific research

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:05.000
and technical communication.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.960
And partly even at the
time we were exclusively

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:09.110
focused on data science.

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:11.680
We sense that the kind
of model we were building

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:14.260
might have broader application,
and we wanted the mission

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:15.650
statement to reflect that.

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:17.200
So that's the charter.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:19.330
But then there's
incentive structures.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:25.510
I would say that many or
most software companies

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:27.520
and technology
companies have kind

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:31.210
of the seeds of their own
demise planted in them

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:32.690
from the earliest days.

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:36.973
Startup companies often exist to
be, really exist, to be acquired.

00:06:36.973 --> 00:06:38.890
And once they're acquired,
all kinds of things

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:40.970
happen to their software
and their mission.

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:45.580
their people. Even public
companies who are not acquired,

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:47.410
who are nominally
independent, are

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:50.680
really, there's a pretty narrow
lane they have to exist in,

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:53.890
which is to always grow,
always grow their revenue,

00:06:53.890 --> 00:06:56.560
always grow their earnings, kind
of at the expense of everything

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:57.060
else.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:01.060
And that certainly also
causes missions to drift.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:03.970
And so the thing that I'm really
pleased about with RStudio

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:06.020
is that we actually are
an independent company.

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:08.500
We're committed to always
being an independent company,

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:11.780
and the control of the company
lies inside the company.

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:13.990
People who work at
RStudio fundamentally

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:15.340
control the company.

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:16.850
It's not controlled
from the outside.

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:19.130
So we actually can
say we're not going

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:21.130
to be, we're going to
continue to be independent.

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:22.430
We can follow through on that.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:26.530
And for us, our imperative, we
certainly would like to grow,

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:28.720
but it's not growth at
all costs and it's never

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:30.880
at the expense of
our core mission.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:34.990
And so our hope maybe
it's a little optimistic,

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:36.730
but we'd like to
be here fulfilling

00:07:36.730 --> 00:07:38.030
that mission in 100 years.

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:41.240
We're certainly going to
think as if we are or try to.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:47.030
And make our work and company
be organically sustainable.

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:51.950
And then finally, there's a
question of scale of effort.

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:56.270
And I'm really happy about
the fact that about 40%

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:58.890
of our engineers work full
time on open source software.

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:02.288
This is a list of some of
our open source projects

00:08:02.288 --> 00:08:03.830
and the number of
full time engineers

00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:06.650
that work on them as of now.

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:08.475
That's 43 engineers.

00:08:08.475 --> 00:08:09.600
But there's more than that.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.480
We actually have
dozens of people

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:14.600
at the company who are not
full time open source software

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:17.780
engineers who also write
packages and contribute

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:18.960
to open source projects.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:20.690
So there's many
more than the 43,

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:23.510
but there's 43 who
are paid to do nothing

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:25.270
but work on open
source software.

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:26.840
43 is great.

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:30.560
I hope in five years
that's going to be 143

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:31.710
and I hope in 10 years.

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:33.450
That's going to be 200 or 300.

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:35.750
And I think that
represents an opportunity

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:39.169
to create a scale of effort
that is really remarkable

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:42.650
and actually presents
an opportunity.

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:45.830
Talking a little bit about how
we kind of got to this scale

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:47.490
with the basic
mechanic is, and I

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:49.490
think I talked about this
a couple of years ago,

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:54.260
to briefly recap,
recount it, there's

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:56.330
a virtuous cycle,
synergistic relationship

00:08:56.330 --> 00:08:58.770
between our open source and
our commercial software.

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:01.320
We write open source tools that
are accessible to everyone.

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:03.170
They're accessible
to anyone independent

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:04.500
of their economic means.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:05.600
And they're there.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:08.420
They're really
useful as they are,

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:10.640
without purchasing anything.

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:12.800
But what happens
is that inevitably,

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:15.470
when lots and lots of people
adopt open source software,

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:18.590
some of that software is
adopted in complex environments,

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:21.830
larger organizations that
have security or scalability

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:23.370
or integration requirements.

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:25.910
People want to use this
software, deploy the software

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:27.480
and use the software on the web.

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:29.180
And that creates an
opportunity for us

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:31.700
to create commercial
products, which

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:34.880
then help grow the company
and allow us to invest back

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:35.970
in our open source tools.

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:38.000
Now, I want to tie
this back a little

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:41.010
bit to the stuff about
mission and incentives.

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:42.480
You'll notice
there's a line here.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:44.522
There's a line between
open source and enterprise

00:09:44.522 --> 00:09:46.950
products and a company,

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:49.550
and we've seen this we've seen
this historically, companies

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:53.300
whose principal imperative
is growth at any cost

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:56.130
actually have an incentive
to move that line over time.

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:58.820
And what I think is great
about what we've done,

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:00.200
because we have
the independence,

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:02.750
is that we actually have an
operative principle for what's

00:10:02.750 --> 00:10:04.260
open source and what is not.

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:07.670
And that is really that the
core intellectual property

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:13.130
that you create - the reports,
applications, analyses, code - that

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:16.190
is all based on open
source packages,

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:18.650
protocols, file formats and
even the core productivity

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:20.810
tools like RStudio
are open source

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:22.290
so that anyone can use them.

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:24.050
And then when we get
into these scenarios

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:26.540
of more complex
environments, that's

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:28.760
when, or deployment
on the web, that's

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:31.320
when commercial software
comes into play.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:33.680
But we will always
respect that line

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:36.950
and never compromise that
line for the sake of,

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:41.245
of goosing our growth for any
given year or period of time.

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:45.150
And I apologize.

00:10:45.150 --> 00:10:47.240
actually, I don't
think the type here

00:10:47.240 --> 00:10:50.750
is going to be super legible
for people past the 10th row.

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:53.660
But this is just a picture of
the company over time, kind

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:55.020
of how this has played out.

00:10:55.020 --> 00:11:00.800
In 2009, it was one person
four years later, in 2013,

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:03.080
there were six
people just working

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:04.207
on open source software.

00:11:04.207 --> 00:11:06.290
And then about five years
into the company's life,

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:09.267
we decided that we'd like to try
to grow this group and do more.

00:11:09.267 --> 00:11:10.850
As you can see, we
started introducing

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:13.220
some commercial products
that actually worked

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:16.160
and we became
profitable very quickly,

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:19.160
and we've managed to grow
really, really substantially

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:22.250
since then and create a lot of,

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:24.830
this is just, this is only some
of the open source software

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:25.740
that we've created.

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:27.980
And as you can see
also, we've managed

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:30.543
to fill in additional
commercial products for all

00:11:30.543 --> 00:11:32.210
the different scenarios
that people have

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:35.600
and that these things,
again, are feeding each other

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:39.380
and allow us to get to
this scale of roughly now

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:43.520
about 250 people in the company
and dozens and dozens of people

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:46.040
working full time on
open source software.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:49.580
So where does this leave us?

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:52.440
We talked about that's
kind of RStudio 2022.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:55.320
And we talked about where
do we go in the future?

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:58.877
And I think what's remarkable
to me is that we have actually

00:11:58.877 --> 00:12:00.710
built this company that
can have open source

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:03.260
software for
science at its core,

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:05.030
and it's able to
fulfill that mission

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:06.590
as an independent company.

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:11.480
And we're able to employ lots
and lots of people working

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:13.280
on open source software
and the potential

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:15.770
to employ many, many more.

00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:17.270
But when we wrote
that mission, we

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:20.220
included scientific research
and technical communication.

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:22.880
And as you've
probably already seen

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:25.430
with Quarto, which you'll
hear more about tomorrow,

00:12:25.430 --> 00:12:26.930
we're starting to
work in that realm

00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:28.050
of scientific communication.

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:29.717
It certainly overlaps
with data science,

00:12:29.717 --> 00:12:31.260
but it goes
significantly beyond it.

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:33.510
So we've been asking ourselves
the question and really

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:35.600
when we started, we
worked on that mission

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:39.050
and we worked on Quarto, you
know, do we have the potential

00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:40.730
does the company
have the potential

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:42.770
to impact the
practice of science

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:44.750
more broadly than
just the practice

00:12:44.750 --> 00:12:46.047
of data science with R?

00:12:46.047 --> 00:12:48.380
And I think the fact that we
wrote the mission that way,

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:49.820
the fact that a
lot of the things

00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:52.153
you've seen us working on
over the last couple of years,

00:12:52.153 --> 00:12:55.010
to me the answer to that is
pretty decisively that we can

00:12:55.010 --> 00:12:57.910
and we should do that.

00:12:57.910 --> 00:12:59.920
So to sum up.

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:02.020
We think we've got a
really good foundation

00:13:02.020 --> 00:13:04.630
towards a sustainable model
for funding open source,

00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:05.840
scientific software.

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:08.980
And we want to do that by
creating this long term,

00:13:08.980 --> 00:13:13.450
thriving, trustworthy company
that's hopefully going to be

00:13:13.450 --> 00:13:16.720
around 100 years from now.

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:19.540
And 100 years is a long time.

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:22.930
Like programming has only
been around for 70 years.

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:26.890
Data science, charitably,
has been around for 30 years.

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:30.090
The languages today like
R and Julia and Python.

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:32.620
Are they going to be popular
even in 30 years or 50

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:34.790
years, let alone 100 years?

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:38.080
So it's pretty clear that
as much as we individually

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:41.770
love specific programming
languages to thrive

00:13:41.770 --> 00:13:43.840
in the long term, the
mission of the company

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:46.930
has to be broader than that.

00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:49.300
And I think you can kind
of see that today already

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:52.570
like one language is not
enough for most data scientists

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:54.160
because most data
scientists have

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:57.370
to use SQL and something else.

00:13:57.370 --> 00:14:01.070
And many teams use a
combination of R and Python.

00:14:01.070 --> 00:14:03.698
And as soon as those
teams want to interop

00:14:03.698 --> 00:14:05.740
with the rest of the
company, even more languages

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:07.720
come onto the table.

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:10.930
And we believe this is
a fundamental property

00:14:10.930 --> 00:14:12.270
of software.

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:15.360
Like, there's never going to be
one language to rule them all.

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:20.330
And so the best tools have
to take this into account.

00:14:20.330 --> 00:14:23.170
And I think we're already
seeing this in open source tools

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:25.540
like Torch and Tensorflow,
which are tools

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:28.930
for doing deep learning, machine
learning, where you express

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:32.950
your model in a human friendly
language, a language designed

00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:35.360
to interact with humans
like R or Python.

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:37.210
And behind the
scenes, your model

00:14:37.210 --> 00:14:40.210
is
converted into something

00:14:40.210 --> 00:14:42.060
very high performance.

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:44.850
Well, Stan, very similar
to Torch and Tensorflow,

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:48.960
but more statistical
computing environment really

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:51.580
helps fit many Bayesian models.

00:14:51.580 --> 00:14:54.840
You define your model in a high
level language like R or Python

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:57.450
or Stata or Matlab, and
then behind the scenes

00:14:57.450 --> 00:15:00.640
that's converted to
something very performant.

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:04.750
Or Arrow by our friends
at Voltron Data. Arrow

00:15:04.750 --> 00:15:08.590
is a data format designed so
that different languages can

00:15:08.590 --> 00:15:12.672
collaborate on literally
the same data in memory.

00:15:12.672 --> 00:15:14.380
So there's none of
this kind of expensive

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:17.890
copying or transforming that
the different languages can

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:21.820
collaborate on exactly
the same data set.

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:26.920
And we're very much inspired
by R. R is not a language

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:32.050
like driven by purity
of its philosophy. R

00:15:32.050 --> 00:15:35.890
is a language designed
to get shit done.

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:39.200
And it's been this, it's
been like this from day one.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:41.410
Yes, the precursor
to R was designed

00:15:41.410 --> 00:15:43.160
by statisticians at Bell Labs.

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:44.860
It had all these
little Fortran programs

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:47.200
that each tackled a
little bit of the data

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:49.150
science of the statistical
analysis problem,

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:52.220
but they needed some tool
kit to join them together.

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:55.060
So R, from day one has
always been about building

00:15:55.060 --> 00:15:59.700
bridges, combining tools
to get things done.

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:02.520
And even our pro products are
kind of already multilingual,

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:04.270
like RStudio Workbench.

00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:06.510
Of course, it
supports RStudio IDE,

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:09.510
but it also supports Jupyter
notebooks, Jupyter lab and Visual

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:12.250
Studio code. RStudio Connect,

00:16:12.250 --> 00:16:15.330
of course, it can deploy Shiny
apps and R Markdown documents,

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:18.390
but it can also deploy Jupyter
notebooks and Flask APIs

00:16:18.390 --> 00:16:20.450
and Dash and Streamlit apps.

00:16:20.450 --> 00:16:24.150
RStudio Package Manager
is not just about R packages,

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:27.300
it's also about Python packages.

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:29.490
But there's something
about those names.

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:33.180
Like, RStudio was a great
name for the company

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:38.520
when our one product was literally
a RStudio for writing R code.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:40.890
But over the last
few years, that name

00:16:40.890 --> 00:16:44.250
has started to feel
increasingly constraining.

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:48.750
So today, I'm very
excited to announce

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:50.330
that RStudio is becoming.

00:16:55.690 --> 00:17:15.250
Posit. So posit is a real word.

00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:17.369
Admittedly, it's a bit
of an obscure word,

00:17:17.369 --> 00:17:20.819
but it means to
put forth an idea

00:17:20.819 --> 00:17:23.079
to pretend that
something is true.

00:17:23.079 --> 00:17:25.410
So that you can argue about it.

00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:27.690
And this is like literally
what data scientists do.

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:29.500
They posit hypotheses.

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:33.698
So that they can test or
evaluate them with data.

00:17:33.698 --> 00:17:34.990
So why do we like this name?

00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:37.500
While it's a real word
and it's authentic, like,

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:39.880
it's literally what you all do.

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:41.290
It's specific.

00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:43.390
It's not some, like,
gobbledygook series

00:17:43.390 --> 00:17:45.190
of letters that
could mean anything,

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:47.560
but it also feels
kind of generic enough

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:50.350
to last us for a
long time to come.

00:17:50.350 --> 00:17:53.650
And if you know anything about
my personal package naming

00:17:53.650 --> 00:17:56.230
philosophy, you'll also
know it makes me very happy

00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:58.100
that it's exactly
five letters long.

00:18:00.970 --> 00:18:03.340
So as well as the
new name posit,

00:18:03.340 --> 00:18:07.210
we have a new logo which
evokes the greater than or less

00:18:07.210 --> 00:18:09.340
than symbols of statistics
and mathematics,

00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:12.250
the angled brackets of
HTML that greater than sign

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:15.020
that you see the
console all the time.

00:18:15.020 --> 00:18:17.950
But also this idea
that weaving together

00:18:17.950 --> 00:18:19.810
different tools,
different languages,

00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:22.450
different environments,
different skills,

00:18:22.450 --> 00:18:24.450
is stronger than they are alone.

00:18:26.990 --> 00:18:28.730
So how do we get this name?

00:18:28.730 --> 00:18:30.240
It was a lot of work.

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:32.450
We partnered with
a branding agency

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:35.450
who did a lot of research,
talking to RStudio employees

00:18:35.450 --> 00:18:37.220
and customers,
community members,

00:18:37.220 --> 00:18:39.790
trying to figure out like
who we are as a company.

00:18:39.790 --> 00:18:41.130
What's our DNA?

00:18:41.130 --> 00:18:43.290
Then that went on to a
bunch of brainstorming.

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:46.190
I think they brainstormed
over 500 names that

00:18:46.190 --> 00:18:48.900
got weeded down to about
80, which we looked at.

00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:52.340
We shortlisted about five, kind
of sat on it for about a week,

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:54.110
but Posit
very, very quickly,

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:57.490
kind of rose to the top.

00:18:57.490 --> 00:18:59.500
So what does this mean?

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:01.130
Well for our Pro products,

00:19:01.130 --> 00:19:03.250
not much, as I
said, they already

00:19:03.250 --> 00:19:05.060
support multiple languages.

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:06.980
They're not
really going to change.

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:09.430
But hopefully now it's
a little bit easier

00:19:09.430 --> 00:19:13.180
to tell that multilingual story
if you're an R user embedded

00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:15.602
in a Python heavy
team or org, hopefully

00:19:15.602 --> 00:19:17.560
it's a little bit easier
to get your colleagues

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:19.090
to take a look at
Posit than it would

00:19:19.090 --> 00:19:21.790
be to get them to take
a look at RStudio.

00:19:21.790 --> 00:19:23.648
And over time, in
the coming months,

00:19:23.648 --> 00:19:25.940
the names of our products
will change Posit Connect,

00:19:25.940 --> 00:19:30.210
Posit Workbench, Posit
Package Manager and so on.

00:19:30.210 --> 00:19:32.280
What about for open source?

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:34.650
Well, the IDE is
already multilingual,

00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:37.740
not just R and Python,
but it supports C and C++

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:40.280
and JavaScript and HTML
and a bunch of other stuff.

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:41.380
Keep doing that.

00:19:41.380 --> 00:19:43.920
But we're also going to
increase our investments

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:47.070
in Visual Studio code.

00:19:47.070 --> 00:19:49.930
But R markdown, it is
evolving to Quarto.

00:19:49.930 --> 00:19:53.190
You're going to hear a lot
about Quarto at this conference.

00:19:53.190 --> 00:19:55.320
I highly recommend
you come back tomorrow

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:57.030
for Julia and Mine's
keynote, which

00:19:57.030 --> 00:19:58.590
is going to be all about Quarto.

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:00.930
But basically
very, very briefly,

00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:05.700
Quarto is like R Markdown, but
designed from the foundations

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:08.940
to be multi-lingual,
not just R or Python,

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:12.910
but any language that might
come along in the future.

00:20:12.910 --> 00:20:15.230
But tidymodels, mlverse,
MLops.

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:17.890
These are already
fundamentally about connecting

00:20:17.890 --> 00:20:21.130
different systems and
languages and paradigms that

00:20:21.130 --> 00:20:22.260
will keep going.

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:24.540
Just be more of the same.

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:25.470
The Shiny.

00:20:25.470 --> 00:20:27.850
Well, that is a
very good question.

00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:29.940
And if you want to
find out the answer,

00:20:29.940 --> 00:20:34.000
you'll have to wait for
Joe's keynote this afternoon.

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:35.360
What about the tidyverse?

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:38.980
Well, I still believe that
R is the best language

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:40.750
for interactive data science.

00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:43.090
We're not going to
stop investing in it.

00:20:43.090 --> 00:20:46.390
I will learn a little
bit about Python,

00:20:46.390 --> 00:20:49.030
but I'm not going to stop,

00:20:49.030 --> 00:20:50.175
stop writing R code.

00:20:54.660 --> 00:20:56.410
But of course, we're
also going to broaden

00:20:56.410 --> 00:20:58.870
our focus a little bit,
start thinking, start doing

00:20:58.870 --> 00:21:01.090
a little experiment about
how we can help folks

00:21:01.090 --> 00:21:02.900
in other language communities.

00:21:02.900 --> 00:21:05.440
And you can expect us
to hire more people who

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:07.430
are multilingual.

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:09.687
Now, most importantly,
the question

00:21:09.687 --> 00:21:11.270
you've all been
asking yourself, well,

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:13.070
what does this
mean for stickers?

00:21:13.070 --> 00:21:17.660
Well, most excitingly, if you
look underneath your seat,

00:21:17.660 --> 00:21:18.810
there is both,

00:21:18.810 --> 00:21:20.160
it's really wedged under there,

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:21.993
so you might have to
do this after the talk,

00:21:21.993 --> 00:21:26.330
but you'll find not only a
new Posit sticker, but also

00:21:26.330 --> 00:21:28.730
kind of a commemorative
RStudio sticker

00:21:28.730 --> 00:21:34.490
to support to recognize the
name that stood us in stead

00:21:34.490 --> 00:21:35.245
for so long.

00:21:47.750 --> 00:21:50.090
One lucky audience
member will also

00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:55.250
find a golden ticket, which
is a free registration

00:21:55.250 --> 00:21:56.980
to the conference next year.

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:10.170
We're still pausing for excitement.

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:21.230
Yeah so Hadley talked a
little bit about, you know,

00:22:21.230 --> 00:22:22.940
the current projects
we're working on

00:22:22.940 --> 00:22:25.640
and where those stand and what
you can expect to see over

00:22:25.640 --> 00:22:27.600
the next couple few years.

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:30.960
But I wanted to address
very, very big picture.

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:32.690
Where are we going long term?

00:22:32.690 --> 00:22:34.820
And I think you can
just look at our mission

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:37.550
and see that we've written
it with a much broader scope

00:22:37.550 --> 00:22:38.760
than data science.

00:22:38.760 --> 00:22:40.790
And we would as
we grow, we would

00:22:40.790 --> 00:22:44.480
like to apply this solution
to funding open source

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:46.760
scientific software
as broadly as we can.

00:22:46.760 --> 00:22:50.360
Now, that said, we are
very decisively oriented

00:22:50.360 --> 00:22:52.310
toward data science
today, and that

00:22:52.310 --> 00:22:53.950
will not change any time soon.

00:22:53.950 --> 00:22:57.710
You can see in Quarto, which
overlaps with data science,

00:22:57.710 --> 00:22:59.660
but also gets into
a broader focus

00:22:59.660 --> 00:23:01.095
on scientific communication.

00:23:01.095 --> 00:23:02.720
We'll be investing
in that quite a bit,

00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:06.380
but I would say for the
next five or 10 years,

00:23:06.380 --> 00:23:08.660
I think this is the
areas of investment

00:23:08.660 --> 00:23:12.650
you're going to see us
work within. Now, over time,

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:15.050
over a longer period of
time, we'll certainly

00:23:15.050 --> 00:23:18.935
be looking for opportunities
to do more and contribute more.

00:23:18.935 --> 00:23:20.810
We don't know what that
looks like right now,

00:23:20.810 --> 00:23:24.900
but we'll definitely be
on the lookout for it.

00:23:24.900 --> 00:23:28.800
And so kind of going
back to our journey

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:31.380
and revising the
language a little bit

00:23:31.380 --> 00:23:33.420
to be phrased a
little more generally

00:23:33.420 --> 00:23:38.430
to talk about science for
me and for my colleagues

00:23:38.430 --> 00:23:41.370
at RStudio and the
company, I hope

00:23:41.370 --> 00:23:44.140
for all of you and
the broader R community.

00:23:44.140 --> 00:23:46.590
I am very excited
about the opportunity

00:23:46.590 --> 00:23:50.460
to take all the things that
we love about R and RStudio

00:23:50.460 --> 00:23:51.760
and bring them to everyone.

00:23:51.760 --> 00:23:53.302
That's what I'm
really excited about.

00:23:53.302 --> 00:23:56.100
So I hope we can all hope
we can all do that together.

00:23:56.100 --> 00:23:58.830
We don't have a website
yet for the new company,

00:23:58.830 --> 00:24:01.210
but there's a little bit
of a placeholder website,

00:24:01.210 --> 00:24:02.970
you'll see you'll see there.

00:24:02.970 --> 00:24:04.790
Thanks, J.J.

